  <section class="brx-sec" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a power flush?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The clearest sign is a radiator that is hot at the top and cold at the bottom. One or two of the signs below is enough to get the system checked.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Cold at the bottom.</strong> Sludge is heavier than water. It sits at the base, and hot water flows over the top.</li>
        <li><strong>Some radiators cold, others fine.</strong> Sludge builds up unevenly. Radiators far from the boiler often go first.</li>
        <li><strong>Black water when you bleed a radiator.</strong> Clear water is normal. Black water is iron oxide, and it is going through your boiler.</li>
        <li><strong>The house takes longer to warm up.</strong> Flow slowly gets weaker as pipes clog.</li>
        <li><strong>A banging or kettling boiler.</strong> Water is boiling in a blocked heat exchanger.</li>
        <li><strong>The boiler cuts out on overheat.</strong> It cannot get rid of heat fast enough.</li>
        <li><strong>You bleed radiators every few weeks.</strong> Corrosion inside makes gas, so the air keeps coming back.</li>
        <li><strong>The system is over 10 years old and never cleaned.</strong> Sludge builds up even with no signs.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Cold at the top or cold at the bottom?</h3>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Cold at the top</h3>
          <p>This is usually air. Bleed the radiator with a key. If it keeps coming back, air or gas is getting in, and that is worth a check.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Cold at the bottom</h3>
          <p>This is usually sludge. Bleeding will not fix it. If more than one radiator is cold at the bottom, the whole system needs a flush.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>When a flush will not help:</strong> it will not fix a broken pump, a stuck valve or a failed boiler part. If the system keeps losing pressure, that is a leak. It needs <a href="/leak-detection-liverpool">finding first</a>. If the boiler is over 15 years old and badly corroded, a new one may make more sense. We will tell you honestly.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="what">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What is a power flush?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It is a deep clean of the inside of your central heating. A pump pushes water and cleaner through every radiator, pipe and the boiler at speed, and carries the sludge out.</p>
      <p>The sludge is called magnetite. It is black iron oxide. It forms when the steel inside radiators slowly rusts in the heating water. Over the years it turns into a thick paste. It blocks radiators, makes the pump work harder and clogs the boiler.</p>
      <p>Draining and refilling does not do the same job. Sludge sticks to the bottom of radiators. Only fast water, a cleaner and flushing each radiator on its own will shift it.</p>
      <h3>Why so many Liverpool homes need one</h3>
      <p>Liverpool has lots of Victorian and Edwardian terraces, in places like Aigburth, Toxteth, Wavertree and Anfield. Many had heating put in during the 1970s and 1980s. Most have never had a proper clean. When we open these systems, the water is often jet black.</p>
      <p>The 1930s semis in Childwall, West Derby and Norris Green often have systems from the 1980s or 1990s. Sludge there is usually lighter. A standard flush gives good, steady results.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a power flush work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It takes most of a day for a typical 3-bed home. Here is what happens, from start to finish.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Check and test the water (15 minutes).</strong> We look at the boiler, pipes and radiators. We check them for weak spots. Then we test a sample of the water.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect the machine (30 minutes).</strong> Our Kamco CF90 Quantum2 pump goes on, usually at the heating pump or across a radiator.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the cleaner.</strong> Fernox F3 for a standard clean. Sentinel X400 where the sludge is heavy. We choose after we see the water.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush each radiator (most of the day).</strong> We flush every radiator on its own. We reverse the flow to shift settled sludge. This is the slow part, and the part cheap jobs skip.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush until clear.</strong> The whole system runs until the water comes back clear. Then we test the water again.</li>
        <li><strong>Add inhibitor.</strong> A full dose to BS 7593. It slows new rust and sludge.</li>
        <li><strong>Balance and report (30 minutes).</strong> We balance the radiators so they heat evenly. You get a written report.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Very heavy sludge sometimes needs Sentinel X400 left in for a few weeks, then a return visit. If your system needs this, we tell you before we start and agree any extra cost first.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Know how many radiators you have?</strong> Call and we will give you a fixed price on the phone.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="compare">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Power flush, chemical clean or mains flush: which is right for me?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">An older system with black water and cold radiators needs a power flush. A newer system with mild signs may only need a £195 chemical clean.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Power flush</th><th>Chemical clean</th><th>Mains flush</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>How it works</strong></td><td>A machine forces water and cleaner through at speed</td><td>Cleaner goes round with your own heating pump</td><td>Mains water pushes through each radiator</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Each radiator done on its own?</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>No, all at once</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Heavy sludge</strong></td><td>Shifts it</td><td>Often leaves it behind</td><td>Can leave it behind</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Systems over 10 years old, black water, cold radiators</td><td>Newer systems with mild signs</td><td>Newer, fairly clean systems</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Our price</strong></td><td>From £395</td><td>£195</td><td>Not sold on its own</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A five-year-old system with one cool radiator may not need a full flush. We will tell you which job you need, not sell you the bigger one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="microbore">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do you flush microbore central heating?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. A microbore power flush is a harder job than a standard one, and many engineers turn it down. We do them often.</p>
      <p>Microbore pipe is 8mm or 10mm wide. Standard pipe is 15mm or 22mm. The narrow pipe fills with sludge much faster. To clear it, we use high flow, a stronger dose of cleaner and reverse the flow again and again.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Microbore</th><th>Standard pipe</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Pipe size</strong></td><td>8mm to 10mm</td><td>15mm or 22mm</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Usual build years</strong></td><td>About 1985 to 2000</td><td>Most other times</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>How fast it blocks</strong></td><td>Quickly</td><td>Slowly</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Time on site</strong></td><td>Longer, sometimes much longer</td><td>Easy to predict</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>How to tell if you have microbore</h3>
      <p>Look at the pipe going into a radiator valve. Microbore is much thinner than normal copper pipe. It is often plastic coated and runs from a central point, called a manifold. You see it a lot in late 1980s and 1990s semis around Childwall, Woolton and Gateacre, and in newer housing north of the city in Bootle, Crosby and Formby.</p>
      <p>Two microbore systems can look the same and need very different work. So we usually look at a microbore system before we fix the price. You still get a fixed written price before we start.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="leaks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can a power flush cause leaks?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It can show up a weak spot that was already there. It does not damage a sound system.</p>
      <p>A flush moves water fast. If a radiator is rusted almost through, it can spring a pinhole. An old valve with a worn seal can start to weep. These parts were close to failing anyway. It is better to find them while we are there than on a winter night.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>We check every radiator, valve and joint before we start.</li>
        <li>If something looks weak, we tell you first.</li>
        <li>If a part fails on the day, we price the fix before we do it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If a radiator is past saving, see our <a href="/radiators-liverpool">radiator replacement prices</a>. New radiators start from £245 supplied and fitted.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="new-boiler">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a power flush before a new boiler?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Usually yes, if the new boiler is going onto an old system. Boiler makers need the system cleaned and treated to BS 7593, or they can refuse a warranty claim.</p>
      <p>BS 7593 is the British Standard for treating the water in central heating. Before a new boiler goes on, the old system should be cleaned of sludge. Then it gets a dose of inhibitor. A simple drain and refill does not meet it.</p>
      <p>Makers such as Worcester Bosch and Vaillant ask for this. A new boiler on a dirty system is the quickest way to shorten its life. Keep our written report with your boiler papers.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Getting a new boiler from us?</strong> We check the system as part of every <a href="/boiler-installation-liverpool">boiler installation</a>. If it needs a flush, it is £350 on the same job.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="after">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How long does a power flush last?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Years, if the system is looked after. Rust never fully stops, but inhibitor slows it right down and a filter catches what still forms.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Flush.</strong> Clear the sludge that is there now.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit a magnetic filter.</strong> A MagnaClean Professional2 sits on the pipe back to the boiler and traps new sludge. £150 with a flush.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the inhibitor every year.</strong> We test and top it up at your <a href="/boiler-service-liverpool">boiler service</a>, and empty the filter.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush again only if the signs come back.</strong> A system with a filter can go a long time between flushes.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our power flushes are done by our Gas Safe registered engineers, part of our team led by James. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk/">Gas Safe Register</a>. Every job has a 12-month workmanship guarantee and £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
